          %h2#Idea Idea
          %p
            In general, the term
            %em Weyl algebra
            refers to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/noncommutative+algebra"} noncommutative
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/associative+algebras"} associative algebras
            controlled by
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/canonical+commutation+relations"} canonical commutation relations
            (CCR) which are the hallmark of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+mechanics"} quantum mechanics
          %p
            More specifically, by the Weyl algebra with
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Weyl+relations"} Weyl relations
            one refers to the
            %em exponentiated
            form of these CCR where the algebra generators are (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/linear+representation"} represented
            by)
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/unitary+operators"} unitary operators
            %a{:href => "#Weyl27"} Weyl 1927, pp. 27
            and further highlighted in
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#vonNeumann31"} von Neumann 1931
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Stone-von+Neumann+theorem"} Stone-von Neumann theorem
            directly applies to.
          %h2#definition Definition
          %p
            Given a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field"} field
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi k
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
            = succeed "-th" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            %strong Weyl algebra
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi A
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                      %mo ,
                      %mi k
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A_{n,k}
            is an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/associative+unital+algebra"} associative unital algebra
            over
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi k
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
            generated by the symbols
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi x
                    %mn 1
                  %mo ,
                  %mi …
                  %mo ,
                  %msup
                    %mi x
                    %mi n
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mo ∂
                    %mn 1
                  %mo ,
                  %mi …
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mo ∂
                    %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x^1,\ldots,x^n,\partial_1,\ldots,\partial_n
            modulo the “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/canonical+commutation+relations"}> canonical commutation relations
            ”
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi x
                      %mi i
                    %msup
                      %mi x
                      %mi j
                    %mo =
                    %msup
                      %mi x
                      %mi j
                    %msup
                      %mi x
                      %mi i
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x^i x^j = x^j x^i
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mo ∂
                    %mi i
                  %msub
                    %mo ∂
                    %mi j
                  %mo =
                  %msub
                    %mo ∂
                    %mi j
                  %msub
                    %mo ∂
                    %mi i
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \partial_i\partial_j = \partial_j\partial_i
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mo ∂
                    %mi i
                  %msup
                    %mi x
                    %mi j
                  %mo −
                  %msup
                    %mi x
                    %mi j
                  %msub
                    %mo ∂
                    %mi i
                  %mo =
                  %msubsup
                    %mi δ
                    %mi i
                    %mi j
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \partial_i x^j - x^j \partial_i = \delta_i^j
            (the
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kronecker+delta"} Kronecker delta
          %p
            In
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/characteristic+zero"} characteristic zero
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/regular+differential+operators"} regular differential operators
            on the
            = succeed "-dimensional" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/affine+space"} affine space
          %p
            Sometimes one considers the Weyl algebras over an arbitrary
            = succeed "-algebra" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi k
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi R
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi R
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi A
                      %mrow
                        %mi n
                        %mo ,
                        %mi k
                    %msub
                      %mo ⊗
                      %mi k
                    %mi R
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A_{n,k}\otimes_k R
          %p
            In
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+physics"} quantum physics
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/complex+numbers"} complex numbers
            (see
            = succeed ");" do
              %a{:href => "#RelationToHeisenbergLieAlgebra"} below
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi p
                    %mi j
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p_j
            for
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} i
                  %msub
                    %mo ∂
                    %mi j
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} - \mathrm{i} \partial_j
            (where
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} i
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathrm{i}
            is the imaginary unit).
          %h2#properties Properties
          %h3#RelationToHeisenbergLieAlgebra Relation to Heisenberg Lie algebra
          %p
            Consider the standard
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symplectic+form"} symplectic form
            on the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cartesian+space"} Cartesian space
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi ℝ
                      %mrow
                        %mn 2
                        %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}^{2n}
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symplectic+vector+space"} symplectic vector space
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Heisenberg+Lie+algebra"} Heisenberg Lie algebra
          %p
            Depending on conventions, the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/universal+enveloping+algebra"} universal enveloping algebra
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Heisenberg+Lie+algebra"} Heisenberg Lie algebra
            either already is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Weyl+algebra"} Weyl algebra
            on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn 2
                  %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 2n
            generators or else it becomes so after after forming the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quotient+algebra"} quotient algebra
            in which the central generator is identified with the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/unit+element"} unit element
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ground+field"} ground field
            – whereas in the former case (considered eg. in
            = succeed ";" do
              %a{:href => "#Kravchenko00"} Kravchenko 2000, Def. 2.1
            = succeed ")" do
              %a{:href => "#Bekaert05"} Bekaert 2005, p. 9
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Planck+constant"} Planck constant
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℏ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \hbar
            with the Weyl algebra regarded as a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/formal+deformation+quantization"} formal deformation quantization
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symplectic+manifold"} symplectic manifold
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi ℝ
                      %mrow
                        %mn 2
                        %mi m
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}^{2m}
          %p
            Accordingly, given a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Heisenberg+Lie+n-algebra"}
              Heisenberg Lie
              = succeed "-algebra" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            it makes sense to call its
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/universal+enveloping+E-n+algebra"}
              universal enveloping
              = succeed "-algebra" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi E
                        %mi n
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E_n
            a
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Weyl+n-algebra"}
                  Weyl
                  = succeed "-algebra" do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi n
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
          %h2#related_concepts Related concepts
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Weyl+relations"} Weyl relations
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/canonical+commutation+relations"} canonical commutation relations
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Heisenberg+algebra"} Heisenberg algebra
          %h2#references References
          %p
            The term “Weyl algebra” for algebras freely generated subject to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/canonical+commutation+relations"} canonical commutation relations
            is due to
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jacques+Dixmier"}> Jacques Dixmier
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Sur les algèbres de Weyl
              %strong 96
              (1968) 209-242 &amp;lbrack;
              %a{:href => "http://www.numdam.org/item/?id=BSMF_1968__96__209_0"}> numdam:BSMF_1968__96__209_0
              \&amp;rbrack;
          %p
            (there attributed to a suggestion by
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Irving+Segal"} Irving Segal
          %ul
            %li#Weyl27
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hermann+Weyl"}> Hermann Weyl
              , (36) in:
              = succeed "," do
                %em Quantenmechanik und Gruppentheorie
              %strong 46
              (1927) 1–46 &amp;lbrack;
              %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1007/BF02055756"}> doi:10.1007/BF02055756
              \&amp;rbrack;
          %p
            However, beware that the invention of
            %a{:href => "#Weyl27"} Weyl 1927
            was not the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/canonical+commutation+relations"} canonical commutation relations
            but their exponential reformulation via the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Weyl+relations"} Weyl relations
          %ul
            %li#vonNeumann31
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/John+von+Neumann"}> John von Neumann
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Die Eindeutigkeit der Schrödingerschen Operatoren
                %strong 104
                (1931) 570–578 &amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1007/BF01457956"}> doi:10.1007/BF01457956
                \&amp;rbrack;
              %blockquote
                %p
                  (proving the
                  = succeed ")" do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Stone-von+Neumann+theorem"} Stone-von Neumann theorem
          %p More on the history:
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Severino+C.+Coutinho"}> Severino C. Coutinho
                , Introduction to:
                = succeed "," do
                  %em A primer of algebraic D-modules
                = succeed "," do
                  %strong 33
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1017/CBO9780511623653"} doi:10.1017/CBO9780511623653
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Severino+C.+Coutinho"}> Severino C. Coutinho
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em The Many Avatars of a Simple Algebra
                %strong 104
                7 (1997) 593-604 &amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.2307/2975052"}> doi:10.2307/2975052
                ,
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a{:href => "https://www.jstor.org/stable/2975052"} jstor:2975052
          %p Further discussion (of either notion):
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Alan+Weinstein"}> Alan Weinstein
                , p. 392 of:
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Deformation quantization
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a{:href => "http://www.numdam.org/item/?id=SB_1993-1994__36__389_0"} numdam:SB_1993-1994__36__389_0
            %li#Kravchenko00
              %p
                Olga Kravchenko,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Deformation Quantization of Symplectic Fibrations
                %strong 123
                (2000) 131–165 &amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/math/9802070"}> arXiv:math/9802070
                ,
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1023/A:1002452002677"} doi:10.1023/A:1002452002677
            %li#Bekaert05
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Xavier+Bekaert"}> Xavier Bekaert
                ,
                %em Universal enveloping algebras and some applications in physics
                (2005) &amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "https://cds.cern.ch/record/904799"}> cds:904799
                ,
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a{:href => "https://cds.cern.ch/record/904799/files/cer-002575006.pdf"} pdf
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Markus+Pflaum"}> Markus Pflaum
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em From Weyl quantization to modern algebraic index theory
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Groups and Analysis – The Legacy of Hermann Weyl
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1017/CBO9780511721410.005"} doi:10.1017/CBO9780511721410.005
            %li
              %p
                Jason Gaddis,
                %em The Weyl algebra and its friends: a survey
                \&amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/2305.01609"}> arXiv:2305.01609
                \&amp;rbrack;
          %p
            On
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/continuous+field+of+C-star+algebras"} continuous fields
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Weyl+algebras"} Weyl algebras
            as
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/continuous+deformation+quantizations"} continuous deformation quantizations
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symplectic+vector+spaces"} symplectic
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+vector+spaces"} topological vector spaces
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ernst+Binz"}> Ernst Binz
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Reinhard+Honegger"} Reinhard Honegger
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Alfred+Rieckers"} Alfred Rieckers
              = succeed "," do
                %em Field-theoretic Weyl Quantization as a Strict and Continuous Deformation Quantization
              %strong 5
              (2004) 327–346 &amp;lbrack;
              %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1007/s00023-004-0171-y"}> doi:10.1007/s00023-004-0171-y
              \&amp;rbrack;
          %p
            On Weyl algebras as
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/groupoid+algebras"} groupoid algebras
            being
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/strict+deformation+quantizations"} strict deformation quantizations
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie-Poisson+structures"} Lie-Poisson structures
            given by
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tangent+Lie+algebroids"} tangent Lie algebroids
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Nicolaas+P.+Landsman"}> Nicolaas P. Landsman
              , B. Ramazan, Ex. 11.3 in:
              = succeed "," do
                %em Quantization of Poisson algebras associated to Lie algebroids
              = succeed "," do
                %em Groupoids in Analysis, Geometry, and Physics
              %strong 282
              (2001) &amp;lbrack;
              %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/math-ph/0001005"}> arXiv:math-ph/0001005
              ,
              = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                %a{:href => "http://www.ams.org/books/conm/282"} ams:conm/282
          %p
            On
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/group+algebras"} group algebras
            of (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/underlying"} underlying
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/discrete+group"} discrete
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Heisenberg+groups"} Heisenberg groups
            as
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/strict+deformation+quantizations"} strict deformation quantizations
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/presymplectic+manifold"} pre-
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symplectic+vector+spaces"} symplectic
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+vector+spaces"} topological vector spaces
            by
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/continuous+field+of+C-star+algebras"} continuous fields of
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Weyl+algebras"} Weyl algebras
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ernst+Binz"}> Ernst Binz
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Reinhard+Honegger"} Reinhard Honegger
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Alfred+Rieckers"} Alfred Rieckers
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Infinite dimensional Heisenberg group algebra and field-theoretic strict deformation quantization
                %strong 38
                1 (2007) &amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "https://ijpam.eu/contents/2007-38-1/6/index.html"}> ijpam:2007-38-1/6
                ,
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a{:href => "https://www.ijpam.eu/contents/2007-38-1/6/6.pdf"} pdf
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Reinhard+Honegger"}> Reinhard Honegger
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Alfred+Rieckers"} Alfred Rieckers
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Heisenberg Group Algebra and Strict Weyl Quantization
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Photons in Fock Space and Beyond, Volume I: From Classical to Quantized Radiation Systems
                = succeed "," do
                  %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1142/9789814696586_0023"} doi;10.1142/9789814696586_0023
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1142/9251-vol1"} doi:10.1142/9251-vol1
          %p See also:
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/eom"}> eom
              \: J.-E. Björk,
              %a{:href => "http://eom.springer.de/w/w097670.htm"} Weyl algebra
          %p
            A
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/categorification"} categorification
            of the Weyl algebra is introduced in operadic language in
          %ul
            %li
              Nikita Markarian,
              = succeed "," do
                %em
                  Weyl
                  = succeed "-algebras" do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi n
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
              %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/1504.01931"} arxiv/1504.01931
